------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

The AC42 is a four-door access controller: four card readers, one Linux SoC, one
tamper-evident audit log that is spooled to the cloud. Every badge presentation
has to end up in that log, and the door logic needs to ask "did I just see this
badge somewhere else?" before it unlocks (anti-passback: one card, one person,
one door at a time).

The vendor library gives you this:

    struct Badge {
        uint32_t badge_id;
        uint8_t  door;        /* 0 .. AC42_DOORS-1, AC42_DOORS == 4 */
        uint64_t timestamp;   /* microseconds, reader_now_us() clock */
    };

    int  reader_wait_badge(uint8_t door, struct Badge *out);
    void reader_cancel(uint8_t door);
    int  audit_store_write(const struct Badge *b);
    uint64_t reader_now_us(void);

  reader_wait_badge(door, out)
      Blocks until somebody badges at `door`.
        0   a badge was read, *out is filled
        -1  nothing for you. The vendor does NOT say why: it is either a
            transient read error (~1 call in 64) or your own reader_cancel().
      PER-DOOR STATE: safe to call concurrently for DIFFERENT doors, undefined
      behaviour if two threads are inside it for the SAME door. This one
      sentence decides the thread model — read it twice.

  reader_cancel(door)
      Makes the in-flight wait, and every later wait for that door, return -1
      promptly. Sticky; there is no un-cancel. Callable from another thread.

  audit_store_write(b)
      Appends one record to flash + the cloud spool. Returns 0. SLOW, and NOT
      thread-safe: it owns one append cursor, so exactly one thread may ever be
      inside it.

  Scaled delays. In the field a reader waits seconds to minutes for a person and
  an audit append costs about a second. So the whole thing would take hours to
  test. The harness scales them down: a badge arrives every 2-9 ms, an append
  costs 8 ms, and the "nobody is at the door" wait used by the shutdown test is
  3 s. The ratios are what matter — the readers together produce faster than the
  store can append, exactly as they do during a shift change.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.  A multi-producer / single-consumer audit pipeline.

      int  audit_init(void);
      void audit_deinit(void);
      int  audit_submit(const struct Badge *b);
      uint64_t audit_logged(void);
      uint64_t audit_dropped(void);

  audit_init() owns every thread: one per door (forced by the per-door vendor
  state) feeding ONE audit logger (forced by the single-cursor store) through a
  bounded queue built from a mutex and two condition variables.

  audit_submit() is what a door thread calls. It may block while the queue is
  full, but only for a bounded AUDIT_SUBMIT_WAIT_US — a door thread asleep here
  is a door that has stopped noticing people. After the deadline, drop the
  record and count it (audit_dropped()).

  Ordering guarantee you must provide, and the harness checks exactly this:
  events from the SAME door reach the store in the order that door read them
  (drops may punch holes, nothing may be reordered or invented); across doors
  any interleaving is acceptable.

  audit_deinit() must return promptly even though all four producers are parked
  inside reader_wait_badge(), and must not throw away badges that were already
  read: cancel the readers, wake every condvar waiter, join the producers, let
  the logger drain the queue, join it.

Part 2.  Duplicate suppression and recent history — non-blocking, thread-safe.

      int    audit_is_duplicate(uint32_t badge_id, uint64_t now_us,
                                uint64_t window_us);
      size_t audit_recent_for_door(uint8_t door, struct Badge *out, size_t max);

  audit_is_duplicate() is on the door-open path, so it must be O(1) average with
  a bounded worst case. It returns 1 when the badge's most recent LOGGED
  sighting t satisfies  t <= now_us && now_us - t <= window_us  (both ends
  inclusive), else 0.

  audit_recent_for_door() returns the last AUDIT_RECENT_PER_DOOR badges at one
  door, newest first, into the caller's buffer.

  Reference structures (see audit_log.h for the exact capacities):
    - an open-addressing hash map keyed by badge_id, fixed size, no malloc,
      with TIME-BASED LAZY EVICTION: a slot whose last sighting is older than
      AUDIT_DEDUPE_RETENTION_US is handed to the next insert that probes over
      it. Decide and defend: hash function, collision strategy (linear probing /
      robin hood / chaining), whether you need tombstones, what the
      load-factor limit means when you cannot rehash, and what happens when a
      probe window is completely full.
    - one fixed ring of the last K badges per door.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what the harness prints PASS/FAIL for)

  - empty state right after audit_init(): no history, nothing is a duplicate,
    bad door / NULL buffer / max == 0 all return 0
  - 1.2 s of load with 3 extra threads hammering the getters: no torn,
    out-of-range or misordered record ever comes out, getter latency stays
    microseconds while the store is busy 8 ms at a time
  - accounting: produced == logged + dropped, exactly; dropped > 0 with a
    16-slot queue in front of an 8 ms store
  - per-door ordering: every logged record is matched against what that door
    produced, in order; one hole is fine, one swap is a FAIL
  - vendor contracts: reader_wait_badge() never entered twice for one door,
    audit_store_write() never entered by a second thread
  - audit_is_duplicate() vs brute force over everything the store received, for
    six window sizes, including exactly-at-the-edge, one microsecond inside the
    edge, window 0, a never-issued badge, and a now_us before the sighting
  - audit_recent_for_door() vs the store's contents for all four doors, for
    max < K and max > K
  - dedupe table: 500 badges, then 500 more a full retention period later must
    recycle the aged-out slots instead of evicting live ones; then 4096 distinct
    recent badges into 1024 slots must stay inside capacity, report the forced
    evictions, keep the most recent badges, and produce no false positives
  - audit_deinit() while all four producers are parked in the vendor call
    returns in well under a second, and nothing is written afterwards

  Last line is exactly "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

